that makes me so successful in my field.

269
00:12:59,490 --> 00:13:02,150
Rajesh, I've had the privilege

270
00:13:02,150 --> 00:13:05,240
of working alongside you for many years.

271
00:13:05,240 --> 00:13:06,880
My recommendation is that

272
00:13:06,880 --> 00:13:09,580
you gobble these up like Tic Tacs.

273
00:13:15,870 --> 00:13:17,790
Okay, I'm still trying to work this out.

274
00:13:17,790 --> 00:13:21,100
How did Penny meet an astronaut?

275
00:13:21,100 --> 00:13:21,960
I don't know.

276
00:13:21,960 --> 00:13:24,380
The regular way people meet astronauts.

277
00:13:26,080 --> 00:13:28,460
Most of those guys live in Texas.

278
00:13:28,460 --> 00:13:31,050
Obviously, this one doesn't.

279
00:13:32,400 --> 00:13:33,370
Okay.

280
00:13:33,370 --> 00:13:35,020
Leonard lives here. Priya's from India.

281
00:13:35,020 --> 00:13:37,400
People meet, Howard. God!

282
00:13:38,650 --> 00:13:39,440
Fine.

283
00:13:39,440 --> 00:13:40,840
You've met lots of astronauts,

284
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:43,060
and I've never grilled you about that.

285
00:13:43,060 --> 00:13:46,190
I'd thank you to extend me the same courtesy.

286
00:13:47,800 --> 00:13:51,250
I'm not grilling you; I was just curious.

287
00:13:51,250 --> 00:13:54,130
I still can't get over the fact that she got a big movie part.

288
00:13:54,130 --> 00:13:58,650
Not that I care what my ex-girlfriend's up to, 'cause I don't.

289
00:13:59,410 --> 00:14:02,650
Maybe that's where she met the astronaut, all right?

290
00:14:02,650 --> 00:14:04,710
I'm sorry. What would an astronaut be doing

291
00:14:04,710 --> 00:14:06,370
working on a movie?

292
00:14:06,930 --> 00:14:09,090
He's a consultant.

293
00:14:10,480 --> 00:14:13,180
I thought the movie was about 18th-century Vienna.

294
00:14:13,180 --> 00:14:15,380
He can't have a hobby?!

295
00:14:17,580 --> 00:14:19,950
Excuse me, I have to pee.

296
00:14:19,950 --> 00:14:22,630
Or is that implausible, as well?!

297
00:14:26,420 --> 00:14:29,630
It's nice to have another couple to hang with, isn't it?

298
00:14:31,610 --> 00:14:33,070
All right, guys, you have to go back.

299
00:14:33,070 --> 00:14:34,480
I can't afford you.

300
00:14:35,170 --> 00:14:38,070
No, don't send us away; we love you.

301
00:14:40,040 --> 00:14:43,340
I love you, too, but you cost more than my rent.

302
00:14:44,460 --> 00:14:46,810
But, Penny, you look so good in us.

303
00:14:47,830 --> 00:14:49,270
Damn it, the shoes are right.

304
00:14:50,370 --> 00:14:52,910
Good golly, Penny, your whimsy is boundless.

305
00:14:57,610 --> 00:14:59,450
What do you got for me, wildebeest?

306
00:15:00,030 --> 00:15:01,840
I think they're on to me.

307
00:15:01,840 --> 00:15:03,950
The story's starting to fall apart.

308
00:15:03,950 --> 00:15:05,910
Calm down. Everything's going to be okay.

309
00:15:05,910 --> 00:15:07,590
We may have to kill her.

310
00:15:08,780 --> 00:15:10,950
Bernadette, I'm putting you on speakerphone.

311
00:15:11,630 --> 00:15:12,770
Where are you now?

312
00:15:12,770 --> 00:15:13,820
In the bathroom.

313
00:15:13,820 --> 00:15:14,760
Oh, look at this,

314
00:15:14,760 --> 00:15:17,310
another one of our classic bathroom gabfests.

315
00:15:18,530 --> 00:15:19,910
What's the problem?

316
00:15:19,910 --> 00:15:20,710
They're just asking me

317
00:15:20,710 --> 00:15:23,010
all sorts of questions I can't answer.

318
00:15:23,010 --> 00:15:24,750
Just change the subject.

319
00:15:24,750 --> 00:15:26,720
I suppose I could get them back to talking about Leonard

320
00:15:26,720 --> 00:15:29,100
going to India to meet Priya's parents.

321
00:15:29,450 --> 00:15:30,800
What?!

322
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:33,070
They said something about going there this summer.

323
00:15:33,070 --> 00:15:34,810
What, are they getting engaged?

324
00:15:34,810 --> 00:15:37,700
I don't know. I was too busy covering my heinie

325
00:15:37,700 --> 00:15:40,180
on Amy's stupid astronaut story!

326
00:15:41,170 --> 00:15:43,080
What astronaut story?

327
00:15:43,080 --> 00:15:45,240
You texted me Penny's dating an astronaut.

328
00:15:45,240 --> 00:15:47,010
I texted "architect." That's amusing.

329
00:15:47,010 --> 00:15:48,960
Auto-correct must've changed it.

330
00:15:50,820 --> 00:15:52,890
Yeah, it's hysterical.

331
00:15:54,520 --> 00:15:56,540
All right, look, just forget about the astronaut.

332
00:15:56,540 --> 00:15:59,870
Architect. Where would you have met an astronaut?

333
00:16:01,020 --> 00:16:03,680
Look, just find out what's up with this trip to India.

334
00:16:03,680 --> 00:16:05,090
I don't want to do this anymore!

335
00:16:05,090 --> 00:16:06,780
Don't you quit on us!

336
00:16:07,610 --> 00:16:09,080
Bernie, you okay?

337
00:16:09,080 --> 00:16:11,570
It was an architect!

338
00:16:14,460 --> 00:16:15,810
Thank you for coming with me, Sheldon.

339
00:16:15,810 --> 00:16:16,970
You're a good friend.

340
00:16:16,970 --> 00:16:18,370
I'm glad you think so.

341
00:16:18,370 --> 00:16:20,630
That's what I strive to emulate.

342
00:16:22,230 --> 00:16:24,730
Okay, let's see if this drug works.

343
00:16:33,100 --> 00:16:34,900
Can I help you?

344
00:16:41,460 --> 00:16:44,590
Forgive me for staring, but you're very beautiful.

345
00:16:51,170 --> 00:16:52,530
Thank you.

346
00:16:52,530 --> 00:16:54,530
That's a great accent. Where are you from?

347
00:16:54,530 --> 00:16:55,380
India.

348
00:16:55,380 --> 00:16:56,880
Oh, cool. I've always wanted to go there.

349
00:16:56,880 --> 00:16:58,830
It's a beautiful country. You'd love it.

350
00:16:58,830 --> 00:17:00,710
May I join you?

351
00:17:00,710 --> 00:17:03,540
Uh, okay, sure. Why not?

352
00:17:03,540 --> 00:17:05,150
My name is Dr. Rajesh Koothrappali,

353
00:17:05,150 --> 00:17:07,440
and this is my friend Dr. Sheldon Cooper.

354
00:17:07,850 --> 00:17:09,240
There's no need to interact with me.

355
00:17:09,240 --> 00:17:11,080
I'm just here to observe.

356
00:17:13,430 --> 00:17:14,400
What's he observing?

357
00:17:14,400 --> 00:17:15,970
We're scientists. We observe everything.

358
00:17:15,970 --> 00:17:17,710
Here, go buy yourself a scone.

359
00:17:19,080 --> 00:17:20,560
All right.

360
00:17:21,740 --> 00:17:23,970
- And what is your name? - Angela.

361
00:17:23,970 --> 00:17:27,540
Oh, derived from the word "angel." Appropriate.

362
00:17:28,040 --> 00:17:29,270
You're cute.

363
00:17:29,270 --> 00:17:31,250
I'd like to buy a scone.

364
00:17:31,250 --> 00:17:32,510
Oh, I'm sorry, we're out.

365
00:17:32,510 --> 00:17:33,760
We have muffins.

366
00:17:33,760 --> 00:17:35,120
They sound delicious,

367
00:17:35,120 --> 00:17:38,010
but this money is earmarked for scones.

368
00:17:38,640 --> 00:17:39,950
What are you doing?!

369
00:17:39,950 --> 00:17:41,660
Oh, just getting comfortable.

370
00:17:43,900 --> 00:17:45,980
So how long have you lived in Los Angeles?

371
00:17:45,980 --> 00:17:46,640
I...

372
00:17:46,640 --> 00:17:47,880
They were out of scones.

373
00:17:47,880 --> 00:17:49,210
Sheldon, I'm talking to someone.

374
00:17:49,210 --> 00:17:50,600
You're being rude.

375
00:17:50,600 --> 00:17:52,270
I'll bet you're an actress.

376
00:17:52,270 --> 00:17:53,490
If not, you should be.

377
00:17:53,490 --> 00:17:55,170
You have a very expressive face.

378
00:17:56,400 --> 00:17:57,670
Oh, my God.

379
00:17:57,670 --> 00:17:59,510
Wait, where are you going?

380
00:17:59,510 --> 00:18:00,970
We were doing so well.

381
00:18:01,580 --> 00:18:03,760
She never even got to see my penis.

382
00:18:16,110 --> 00:18:17,660
Who wants some more coffee?

383
00:18:17,660 --> 00:18:19,230
- Thanks, yeah. - Sure.

384
00:18:19,230 --> 00:18:20,410
Let me help you.

385
00:18:22,490 --> 00:18:25,080
Nice to see that the gals are getting along.

386
00:18:26,330 --> 00:18:27,700
"Gals"?

387
00:18:27,700 --> 00:18:29,800
Who are you, Fred Flintstone?

388
00:18:31,550 --> 00:18:33,250
This trip to India sounds fun.

389
00:18:33,250 --> 00:18:34,990
Yeah, I think it will be.

390
00:18:34,990 --> 00:18:37,850
Are you concerned your parents might not approve of Leonard?

391
00:18:37,850 --> 00:18:38,990
A little.

392
00:18:38,990 --> 00:18:40,640
They're very old-fashioned.

393
00:18:40,640 --> 00:18:42,410
Well, I wouldn't worry about it.

394
00:18:42,410 --> 00:18:45,040
It's not like you guys are getting engaged, right?

395
00:18:47,930 --> 00:18:50,330
Oh, God, no. We're not there yet.

396
00:18:50,330 --> 00:18:53,290
Not engaged. Very interesting.

397
00:18:53,290 --> 00:18:54,380
I have to tinkle.

398
00:18:54,380 --> 00:18:56,200
You've been in there a lot tonight. Are you okay?

399
00:18:56,200 --> 00:18:58,880
Yes. Are you writing a book?

400
00:19:00,500 --> 00:19:01,950
Why are you getting upset?

401
00:19:01,950 --> 00:19:04,070
I'm not upset. Maybe you're upset.

402
00:19:05,310 --> 00:19:07,160
What's up with you?

403
00:19:10,070 --> 00:19:11,510
Bernadette?

404
00:19:13,220 --> 00:19:15,290
I can't do this anymore!

405
00:19:15,290 --> 00:19:16,410
I'm a good girl!

406
00:19:16,410 --> 00:19:18,560
I went to Catholic school!

407
00:19:20,890 --> 00:19:22,680
Okay, well, it's getting late.

408
00:19:22,680 --> 00:19:25,070
This was terrific.

409
00:19:25,070 --> 00:19:26,790
You win.

410
00:19:27,510 --> 00:19:28,700
Bernie?

411
00:19:32,020 --> 00:19:34,480
I think the word you're looking for is "befuddled."

412
00:19:39,880 --> 00:19:45,180
My catapult flings my bishop

413
00:19:45,180 --> 00:19:48,710
to Howard's Queen's Gorilla two.

414
00:19:50,620 --> 00:19:51,400
Nice.

415
00:19:51,400 --> 00:19:55,740
Okay, Rook to transporter pad.

416
00:19:57,160 --> 00:20:01,560
And he comes out at...

417
00:20:01,560 --> 00:20:04,560
...Leonard's Queen's Bishop five-and-a-third.

418
00:20:04,560 --> 00:20:06,320
Check on Leonard.

419
00:20:08,090 --> 00:20:09,880
Hang on.

420
00:20:09,880 --> 00:20:13,010
When is my Pawn allowed to use the golf cart?

421
00:20:14,410 --> 00:20:15,900
When it's done charging.

422
00:20:15,900 --> 00:20:17,810
Or you land on the time machine.

423
00:20:17,810 --> 00:20:19,760
Obviously.

424
00:20:23,250 --> 00:20:27,210
Beekeeper to King 12.

425
00:20:27,210 --> 00:20:31,560
I capture your Pope and release the Swarm.

426
00:20:33,360 --> 00:20:35,920
Checkmate on Sheldon.

427
00:20:35,920 --> 00:20:39,590
I knew I should've given my Pope the jet pack.

428
00:20:40,430 --> 00:20:42,420
Hey, I've got winners.

